        <!-- Section 01: The Philosophy -->
        <section id="philosophy">
          <span class="step-badge">STAGE 01</span>
          <h2>The Editorial Philosophy</h2>
          <p>
            Most modern websites resemble generic marketing templates: identical 3-column card grids, centered 50/50 splits, and arbitrary whitespace. Editorial design—the discipline seen in classic publications, architectural monographs, and Swiss exhibition posters—operates on very different principles:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Asymmetry with Intent:</strong> An asymmetric 7:5 or 8:4 division creates optical momentum and guides the reader's eye forward through the narrative.</li>
            <li><strong>Proportional Harmony:</strong> Layout dimensions are derived from classical geometric ratios (such as the Golden Ratio <code>1:1.618</code> and the Japanese Silver Ratio <em>Hakugin-hi</em> <code>1:1.414</code>) rather than arbitrary pixel numbers.</li>
            <li><strong>Shared Vertical Baselines:</strong> Multi-column cards and adjacent text blocks align to horizontal grid lines across the page, maintaining typographic calm even when content lengths vary.</li>
            <li><strong>Negative Space as an Element (余白 Yohaku):</strong> Empty space is not "unused space"—it is the structural boundary that gives the written word its authority and clarity.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            In this walkthrough, we will build <strong>The Lattice Review</strong>, a cultural monograph exploring the historic zen gardens and pavilion architectures of Kyoto, Japan. You can test each stage across Desktop, Tablet, and Mobile viewports using the controls on each preview.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 02: Step 1 - The Publication Shell -->
        <section id="step-1">
          <span class="step-badge">STAGE 02 · STEP 1</span>
          <h2>The Publication Shell & Fluid Container</h2>
          <p>
            Every Kōshi project begins by importing the core stylesheet. Kōshi is enclosed in <code>@layer kos-grid</code>, which ensures you can write custom typography and brand styles without fighting specificity wars.
          </p>
          <p>
            Next, we establish our document boundaries using <code>.kos-container</code> and add a publication masthead. Unlike fixed-margin wrappers, Kōshi calculates its edge offsets using a fluid clamp formula: <code>clamp(1rem, 3.5vw, 3.5rem)</code>.
          </p>

          <!-- Step 1 Website Preview with Responsive Iframe -->
          <div class="browser-preview" data-browser-preview>
            <div class="browser-preview__chrome">
              <div class="browser-preview__dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
